  "_comment": [
    "Rules for tithi-based (lunar) festivals. Each entry says which lunar month,",
    "which half of it, which tithi, and at what moment of the day the tithi must be",
    "running for the festival to fall on that day. From these ncal computes the date",
    "for any BS year in range — there is no per-year table to maintain.",
    "",
    "'lunar_month' is 1-12 and names the AMANTA month: the lunation from one new",
    "moon to the next, named after the BS solar month the opening new moon falls in.",
    "Nepali speech and most printed patros use PURNIMANTA names, in which a krishna",
    "paksha carries the NEXT month's name — Maha Shivaratri is spoken of as Falgun",
    "krishna 14 but is written here as Magh krishna 14. To translate a purnimanta",
    "rule: a shukla paksha keeps its month number, a krishna paksha loses one.",
    "Amanta is used because it is the scheme that closes over a single lunation and",
    "therefore cannot drift across a sankranti.",
    "",
    "'kala' is the instant the tithi is judged at:",
    "  udaya     - sunrise. The default; the tithi at daybreak owns the day.",
    "  madhyanna - midday, the third fifth of daylight. Bhai Tika, Ram Navami.",
    "  aparahna  - the fourth fifth of daylight. Vijaya Dashami is aparahna-vyapini.",
    "  pradosh   - dusk, shortly after sunset. Laxmi Puja's amavasya, Chhath's arghya.",
    "  nishita   - the solar midnight opening the day. Shivaratri, Janmashtami.",
    "  begins    - not an instant: the day the tithi starts on. Currently unused.",
    "",
    "A date the government moves for its own reasons goes in festivals.json's",
    "'lunar' array, which overrides whatever is computed here for that BS year.",
    "",
    "'spans' are the gazetted holiday blocks: every day from the date 'from' resolves",
    "to through 'to' plus 'extra_days' is a public holiday. Dashain runs Fulpati to",
    "Dwadashi, Tihar from Laxmi Puja to the day after Bhai Tika. Days in between that",
    "carry no holiday of their own get the span's name."
  ],

